==English==

===Etymology===
With a secondary frequentative suffix {{m|en|-er}}, ultimately pointing to {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*hankōną}}, an iterative to {{m|gem-pro|*hanhaną||to hang}}. Related to {{cog|nl|hunkeren||to crave}}, which continues the zero-grade iterative.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|æŋkə(r)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[crave]], [[want]] or [[desire]].
#: ''If you '''hanker''' for chocolate, you'll like this fudge recipe.''
#* '''2012''', The Economist, 13 Oct 2012, ''[http://www.economist.com/node/21564544 Butlers: Very good, sir]''
#*: [...] the newly rich '''hanker''' after old aristocratic glitz.

====Usage notes====
* Usually used with [[for]], as in the example above; [[after]] may also be used.

